s6-2-p13 : Block
s6-2-p13 = prose (cons "The implementation-level `residual` operation above acts on represented arguments. Its general calculation is the staged specialization of represented functions. To keep the levels explicit, write \(C(A)\) for closed checked code of type \(A\), \(q_A(a):C(A)\) for an *available* representation of a value, and \(q_{C(A)}(c)\) for a representation of the **code package as data**, not its evaluation. Let" (cons "\[" (cons "S_{A,B,D}:C(A\to B\to D)\to C(A)\to C(B\to D)" (cons "\]" (cons "be a specializer equipped with the equation" (cons "\[" (cons "\llbracket S_{A,B,D}(e,q_A(a))\rrbracket(b)" (cons "=\llbracket e\rrbracket(a)(b)." (cons "\tag{6.F1}" (cons "\]" (cons "The equality is a path of meanings where required; this notation suppresses that witness, not its existence. For an interpreter \(e:C(P\to X\to Y)\) and represented source program \(q_P(p)\), the **first projection** is" (cons "\[" (cons "r_p=S_{P,X,Y}(e,q_P(p)):C(X\to Y),\qquad" (cons "\llbracket r_p\rrbracket(x)=\llbracket e\rrbracket(p)(x)." (cons "\tag{6.F2}" (cons "\]" (cons "To specialize the *specializer* rather than the interpreted program, set" (cons "\[" (cons "A_0=C(P\to X\to Y),\quad B_0=C(P),\quad D_0=C(X\to Y)," (cons "\]" (cons "and take a checked presentation \(s:C(A_0\to B_0\to D_0)\) of the selected algorithm \(S_{P,X,Y}\). The **second projection** is" (cons "\[" (cons "c_e=S_{A_0,B_0,D_0}(s,q_{A_0}(e)):C(B_0\to D_0)," (cons "\quad" (cons "\llbracket c_e\rrbracket(q_P(p))=S_{P,X,Y}(e,q_P(p))." (cons "\tag{6.F3}" (cons "\]" (cons "The static input here is *the interpreter code*; the dynamic input is *the source-program code*. For the **third projection**, let" (cons "\[" (cons "A_1=C(A_0\to B_0\to D_0),\quad B_1=C(A_0),\quad D_1=C(B_0\to D_0)," (cons "\]" (cons "and let \(s':C(A_1\to B_1\to D_1)\) present the specialization algorithm at this next code type. Then" (cons "\[" (cons "g=S_{A_1,B_1,D_1}(s',q_{A_1}(s)):C(B_1\to D_1)," (cons "\quad" (cons "\llbracket g\rrbracket(q_{A_0}(e))=c_e." (cons "\tag{6.F4}" (cons "\]" (cons "These are typed instances of one residualization equation, not three new compiler operators. In particular, \(q_{A_0}(e)\) and \(q_{A_1}(s)\) quote finite code *as data* at the next stage; they do not posit an unrestricted function \(A\to C(A)\). The displayed equalities express semantics; the actual elimination of interpreter dispatch additionally depends on the selected specializer reducing operations on static constructors while retaining unknown inputs. This is the same distinction exhibited concretely by `apply-now`, `normalize-held`, and the arithmetic residual above. The familiar abbreviations \(S(E,p)\), \(S(S,E)\), and \(S(S,S)\) are valid only with these code-level arguments and stage indices understood." nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
